        <ns1:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>&apos;&apos;</ns1:EMMO_33ae2d07_5526_4555_a0b4_8f4c031b5652>
        <ns1:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>\&quot;</ns1:EMMO_7f1dec83_d85e_4e1b_b7bd_c9442d4f5a64>
        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">&quot;Arc Second&quot; is a unit of angular measure, also called the `second of arc`, equal to 1/60 arcminute. One arcsecond is a very small angle: there are 1,296,000 in a circle. The SI recommends `double prime` (&apos;&apos;) as the symbol for the arcsecond. The symbol has become common in astronomy, where very small angles are stated in milliarcseconds (mas).

-- QUDT</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
        <ns1:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minute_of_arc#Symbols.2C_abbreviations_and_subdivisions</ns1:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
