    <!-- https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0bb3b434_73aa_428f_b4e8_2a2468648e19 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0bb3b434_73aa_428f_b4e8_2a2468648e19">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_f1025834_0cd2_42a1_bfeb_13bec41c8655"/>
        <rdfs:comment>A material is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern.

A solid is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering. In all cases, the positions of the diffraction peaks can be expressed by


H=∑ni=1hia∗i  (n≥3)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/materials#"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Crystal</rdfs:label>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Crystal</skos:prefLabel>
        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>A material is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern.

A solid is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering. In all cases, the positions of the diffraction peaks can be expressed by


H=∑ni=1hia∗i  (n≥3)</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0bb3b434_73aa_428f_b4e8_2a2468648e19"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">Crystal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <ns1:EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25 xml:lang="en">From Ancient Greek κρύσταλλος (krústallos, “clear ice”), from κρύος (krúos, “frost”).</ns1:EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_0bb3b434_73aa_428f_b4e8_2a2468648e19"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A material is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern.

A solid is a crystal if it has essentially a sharp diffraction pattern. The word essentially means that most of the intensity of the diffraction is concentrated in relatively sharp Bragg peaks, besides the always present diffuse scattering. In all cases, the positions of the diffraction peaks can be expressed by


H=∑ni=1hia∗i  (n≥3)</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">https://dictionary.iucr.org/Crystal</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


