        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">an internal compartment of the electrochemical flow cell through which catholyte flows</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c39b2498_783e_48e1_9814_6164bd99823c -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c39b2498_783e_48e1_9814_6164bd99823c">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_4f2d3939_91b1_4001_b8ab_7d19074bf845"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_aecc6094_c6a5_4a36_a825_8a497a2ae112"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Because the working potential range is a function of a specific electrode/electrolyte system, only approximate ranges can be given for generic systems.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The working potential range is usually limited by the stability of the electrolyte, itself affected by the catalytic properties of the electrode, or by the stability of the electrode.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">range of electrode potentials of a given working electrode in a given electrolyte, where the electric current from reactions of the electrode or electrolyte is negligible compared with the current from reactions of the system under investigation</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry/reference/electrochemistry-quantities#"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">WorkingPotentialRange</rdfs:label>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">WorkingPotentialRange</skos:prefLabel>
        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">range of electrode potentials of a given working electrode in a given electrolyte, where the electric current from reactions of the electrode or electrolyte is negligible compared with the current from reactions of the system under investigation</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c39b2498_783e_48e1_9814_6164bd99823c"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">range of electrode potentials of a given working electrode in a given electrolyte, where the electric current from reactions of the electrode or electrolyte is negligible compared with the current from reactions of the system under investigation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:source>J. M. Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</dcterms:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c3e97d58_1854_4c23_bb42_d2972172865e -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c3e97d58_1854_4c23_bb42_d2972172865e">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_02eb0465_4f94_453c_8c1f_5ddf80134634"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The rate at which the deliverable capacity of an electrochemical cell decreases during open-circuit storage under specified conditions, in the absence of an external load.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry/reference/electrochemistry-quantities#"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">SelfDischargeRate</rdfs:label>
        <skos:note xml:lang="en">Arises from internal parasitic reactions including electrolyte oxidation at the positive electrode, SEI instability at the negative electrode, and electronic leakage through the separator.</skos:note>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">SelfDischargeRate</skos:prefLabel>
        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">The rate at which the deliverable capacity of an electrochemical cell decreases during open-circuit storage under specified conditions, in the absence of an external load.</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
        <ns1:EMMO_b6730304_cabc_4104_b415_14218466445c xml:lang="en">Strongly temperature- and state-of-charge-dependent. Values reported without these conditions specified cannot be meaningfully compared.</ns1:EMMO_b6730304_cabc_4104_b415_14218466445c>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c3f4b34a_0e2c_46f3_baab_4ebd2682d26f -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c3f4b34a_0e2c_46f3_baab_4ebd2682d26f">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_5f9a9411_05f9_4576_acd3_81d7d41cfe98"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">An ionic liquid is an electrolyte composed of a salt that is liquid below 100 °C. Ionic liquids have found uses in electrochemical analysis, because their unconventional properties include a negligible vapor pressure, a high thermal and electrochemical stability, and exceptional dissolution properties for both organic and inorganic chemical species.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/reference/electrochemistry-reference#"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">IonicLiquidElectrolyte</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">IonicLiquid</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">IonicLiquidElectrolyte</skos:prefLabel>
        <ns1:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25 xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Ionic_liquid</ns1:EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25>
        <ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9 xml:lang="en">An ionic liquid is an electrolyte composed of a salt that is liquid below 100 °C. Ionic liquids have found uses in electrochemical analysis, because their unconventional properties include a negligible vapor pressure, a high thermal and electrochemical stability, and exceptional dissolution properties for both organic and inorganic chemical species.</ns1:EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9>
        <ns1:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ionic_liquid</ns1:EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c3f4b34a_0e2c_46f3_baab_4ebd2682d26f"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">An ionic liquid is an electrolyte composed of a salt that is liquid below 100 °C. Ionic liquids have found uses in electrochemical analysis, because their unconventional properties include a negligible vapor pressure, a high thermal and electrochemical stability, and exceptional dissolution properties for both organic and inorganic chemical species.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:source>J. M. Pingarron et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</dcterms:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c4120ce7_f1ff_4917_b59e_002454a65040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_c4120ce7_f1ff_4917_b59e_002454a65040">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo#EMMO_faab3f84_e475_4a46_af9c_7d249f0b9aef"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_aecc6094_c6a5_4a36_a825_8a497a2ae112"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">an indicator of the electric power at which an electrochemical device is operated</rdfs:comment>
